> API and interpretation

The original three classic ciphertext demonstrations remain; RFC6229 adds an
official independently specified keystream control. Empty keys now reject before
a modulo-by-zero/index operation. Raw byte strings preserve embedded NUL/high
bytes. The original key recipe explicitly wraps its 32-bit multiplication before
reducing modulo 1,000,000; no random-key claim is made. The grouped first bin
contains sixteen byte values, so it is not itself the second-byte-zero
frequency. RFC7465 prohibits RC4 cipher suites in TLS. This historical
implementation supplies neither entropy acquisition, nonce management nor
authentication, and its conformance vectors are not a security endorsement.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [RFC6229: Test Vectors for the Stream Cipher RC4|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6229]
- [RFC7465: Prohibiting RC4 Cipher Suites|https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7465]
- [Mantin and Shamir, A Practical Attack on Broadcast RC4 (2001)|https://doi.org/10.1007/3-540-45473-X_13]

